{"type":"standard","title":"Akabo dialect","displaytitle":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Akabo dialect</span></span>","namespace":{"id":0,"text":""},"wikibase_item":"Q35361","titles":{"canonical":"Akabo_dialect","normalized":"Akabo dialect","display":"<span lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><span class=\"mw-page-title-main\">Akabo dialect</span></span>"},"pageid":16912113,"thumbnail":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Languages_and_dialects_of_the_Andaman_Islands_at_British_contact.png/330px-Languages_and_dialects_of_the_Andaman_Islands_at_British_contact.png","width":330,"height":478},"originalimage":{"source":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a9/Languages_and_dialects_of_the_Andaman_Islands_at_British_contact.png","width":700,"height":1014},"lang":"en","dir":"ltr","revision":"1354348008","tid":"15c122c0-50a5-11f1-b87a-04406505eb44","timestamp":"2026-05-15T21:28:59Z","description":"Extinct Great Andamanese language","description_source":"local","content_urls":{"desktop":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akabo_dialect","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akabo_dialect?action=history","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akabo_dialect?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Akabo_dialect"},"mobile":{"page":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akabo_dialect","revisions":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Special:History/Akabo_dialect","edit":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Akabo_dialect?action=edit","talk":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Talk:Akabo_dialect"}},"extract":"Akabo or Bo is an extinct dialect of the Northern Andamanese language. It was spoken on the west central coast of North Andaman and on North Reef Island of the Andaman Islands in India. It was recorded as being mutually intelligible with Aka-Jeru, and the vocabularies are very similar.","extract_html":"<p><b>Akabo</b> or <b>Bo</b> is an extinct dialect of the Northern Andamanese language. It was spoken on the west central coast of North Andaman and on North Reef Island of the Andaman Islands in India. It was recorded as being mutually intelligible with Aka-Jeru, and the vocabularies are very similar.</p>"}